#!/bin/sh
#
# A CLOSING KEYWORD IN A COMMIT MESSAGE, REFUSED WHILE THE COMMIT DOES NOT YET
# EXIST (#1430, Retro run 5's R13).
#
# ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# WHY THIS HOOK AND NOT ANOTHER SENTENCE
#
# #376's rule — never `Closes #N` / `Fixes #N` / `Resolves #N`, because GitHub
# closes the card on merge before the receipt exists — is written down in THREE
# places: the card, `PROGRAM.md`'s own clause, and the night-shift standing
# orders. It was breached SIX times in eight days by shifts that had all three
# on stdin, which made it the most-hit gate rejection of that window.
#
# `.githooks/pre-commit`'s own header already answered this, for two other
# repeats, and its sentence is the one to keep: **a sentence did not hold; a
# sentence will not hold.**
#
# ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# THE DEFECT WAS *WHERE* THE READER SAT, NOT THAT IT WAS MISSING
#
#   reader                          reads                       repair cost
#   ──────────────────────────────  ─────────────────────────   ───────────────
#   this hook                       the message being written   FREE — no commit
#   gate: check-closing-keyword     PR title + body             one edit
#   merge tool: refuseAccidentalClose   title + body + COMMITS  A NEW BRANCH
#
# So a keyword living ONLY in a commit message passed the gate GREEN and was
# caught solely by the last reader, at the one moment the repair is most
# expensive — a pushed commit message cannot be fixed without a force push, and
# that is on the ask list. The measured instance cost FOUR pull requests for one
# word (#1347: the gate reddened on the body, the body was fixed, the merge tool
# still refused on a commit message, the branch had to be rebuilt as #1355, and
# closing #1353 collided with a hand review already posted on its head).
#
# Nothing here moves or loosens the gate's body check: six cheap reds are six
# one-minute repairs, which is that step working as designed.
#
# ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# WHICH ROADS FIRE IT — MEASURED, NOT REASONED (git 2.55.0.windows.5, one
# scratch repository per road). The measurement LIVES in
# server/commitMsgHook.test.ts rather than in this comment: every row below is an
# arm there, and that suite holds this table equal to what it drives, so a git
# upgrade that changes a verdict reddens instead of leaving a stale sentence in a
# header whose whole authority is "measured".
#
#   road                              pre-commit   commit-msg
#   ────────────────────────────────  ──────────   ──────────
#   git commit                        FIRED        FIRED
#   git commit --amend                FIRED        FIRED
#   git merge (a real merge commit)   skipped      FIRED
#   git revert                        skipped      skipped
#   git cherry-pick                   skipped      skipped
#   git rebase (a replayed commit)    skipped      skipped
#   git commit --no-verify            skipped      skipped
#
# ⚠ THE THREE SKIPPED ROADS ARE THE SAME THREE `CLAUDE.md`'s #606 PARAGRAPH
# MEASURED FOR `pre-commit`, AND HERE THEY MATTER LESS — FOR A REASON, NOT BY
# LUCK. All three REPLAY a message that already exists: a revert's generated
# subject is `Revert "<the original subject>"`, and a cherry-pick and a replayed
# rebase commit carry the original verbatim. That original was itself authored
# through `git commit`, where this hook DID run, so a clean history stays clean
# through all three.
#
# ⚠ THE ONE AUTHORING ROAD IT DOES NOT COVER, SAID PLAINLY: a revert or
# cherry-pick whose message a hand EDITS. The words are new and no hook sees
# them. That is exactly why `refuseAccidentalClose` stays in the merge tool —
# this hook makes the common case free, it does not replace the backstop.
#
# ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# ONE READER, NOT A SECOND COPY OF THE PATTERN
#
# `scripts/lib/closingKeyword.mts` stays the only place the keywords and the
# reference forms are written down — a regex in this shell script would be
# working law 4 with a merge queue attached. `check-closing-keyword.mts` already
# takes `--file <path>`, and `commit-msg` is handed the message file as `$1`, so
# this is a call rather than a reimplementation.
#
# It REFUSES when it cannot read (the checker's own contract), which is the right
# direction here: a commit whose message was never checked is the state this hook
# exists to prevent.
#
# `core.hooksPath=.githooks` already arms it for every clone, and the deploy rite
# refuses to run without that, so this needs no new per-clone configuration.
#
# Driven both ways by `server/commitMsgHook.test.ts` against real temporary
# repositories, on the real offending text from #1347.
# ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# The checker is resolved relative to THIS FILE, not to `git rev-parse
# --show-toplevel`, for the same reason ARM 1 of `pre-commit` sources its guard
# from `$(dirname "$0")`: the hook's tooling ships beside the hook. It also means
# a scratch repository can point `core.hooksPath` here and get the real check.
HOOK_DIR=$(CDPATH= cd -- "$(dirname -- "$0")" && pwd)
TOOL_ROOT=$(CDPATH= cd -- "$HOOK_DIR/.." && pwd)

# git hands the message path relative to the repository being committed, so it is
# made absolute BEFORE anything changes directory.
case "$1" in
  /* | [A-Za-z]:[/\\]*) MSG_FILE="$1" ;;
  *) MSG_FILE="$(pwd)/$1" ;;
esac

# A tree without the checker (a scratch clone, a worktree mid-surgery) is not a
# commit to refuse — say so out loud rather than blocking every commit on a
# missing file, and rather than passing silently, which would read as a check.
if [ ! -f "$TOOL_ROOT/scripts/check-closing-keyword.mts" ]; then
  echo "commit-msg: scripts/check-closing-keyword.mts is not beside this hook —"
  echo "            the closing-keyword check was SKIPPED, not passed."
  exit 0
fi

# Run from the tool root so `npx` resolves `tsx` in the repository that owns the
# checker, whatever directory the commit was made from.
if (CDPATH= cd -- "$TOOL_ROOT" && npx tsx scripts/check-closing-keyword.mts --file "$MSG_FILE"); then
  exit 0
fi

cat <<'GUIDANCE'

commit-msg REFUSED this commit (#376, #1430).

A closing keyword before a card number closes the card on merge, before the
receipt that proves the work shipped exists. Write `Card: #N` instead — the
relay closes the card once it has actually shipped.

Nothing has been committed, so this costs one edit to the message. The same word
reaching a pushed commit costs a new branch: a commit message cannot be repaired
without a force push, and the merge tool reads commit messages too.
GUIDANCE

exit 1
